rag=false tok/s=14.2 | Q: Who was Srinivas Ramanujan | A: **Srinivas Ramanujan** (1890–1920) was an **Indian mathematician** renowned for his groundbreaking contributions to **number theory**, **particular functions**, and **special functions**. Born in Madras (now Chennai), he was the son of a Tamil farmer and a Hindu widow, and despite facing social and economic challenges, he excelled academically. | ### Key Contributions: | 1. **The Riemann Zeta Function**: | - Ramanujan derived **modular forms** and **theta functions** related to the Riemann zeta function, which are now fundamental in number theory. | - He proved identities involving the zeta function for negative even integers, a result that remains a cornerstone of mathematical research. | 2. **Special Functions**: | - He introduced **Lambert series**, **theta functions**, and **modular forms**, which have applications in physics, combinatorics, and analysis. | - His work on **modular forms** laid the groundwork for later developments in **modularity theory** and **elliptic curves**. | 3. **Collaboration with G.H. Hardy**: | - Ramanujan corresponded with the British mathematician **G.H. Hardy**, who recognized his genius and invited him to Cambridge to study at the University of Cambridge. | - Their collaboration led to the publication of several influential papers, including the 1917 *Proceedings of the Royal Society* paper on **modular forms**, which won Hardy the **G.B. Tait Prize**. | ### Legacy: | - Ramanujan’s work bridged **pure mathematics** and **applied mathematics**, influencing fields like **quantum physics** and **cryptography**. | - He is celebrated as a symbol of **self-reliance** and **genius**, overcoming poverty and discrimination to achieve global recognition. | - His papers, many of which were never published during his lifetime, are now considered masterpieces of mathematical thought. | Srinivas Ramanujan’s legacy endures as a testament to the power of curiosity, creativity, and perseverance in mathematics.
